{
  "abstract": "We refine the finite-conductor Burgess-to-residue argument of Paper 1/v3 by retaining the number of odd prime factors and replacing a logarithmic moment cap by two explicit admissibility conditions. For a primitive nonprincipal real character of conductor $q$, we give a complete character-sum and Laurent-constant transfer using the imported logarithmic derivative bound $L'/L(1)<(\\log q)/2+8\\log\\log q+45$. A factorial factor-count estimate yields the sufficient all-conductor thresholds $\\log\\log q\\ge67,169,1710$ for $L(1,\\chi)\\log q>2,2.1,2.18$. The same transfer applied to the published composite Theorem 1.1 of Hasanalizade et al. already gives the sufficient ranges 68,177,1792, which accounts for most of the improvement over the older thresholds. When the odd part is prime, moments of order $\\sqrt{\\log q/\\log\\log q}$ prove a continuous-range error bounded by $(21/10)\\sqrt{\\log\\log q/\\log q}$ in the normalized denominator. We explicitly apply the same residue transfer to Treviño's all-moment prime estimate and the cubefree estimate of Hasanalizade et al.; the prime convergence order already follows from the former, and its finite baseline is stronger than our illustrative prime endpoints. Exact rational certificates support parameter choices and endpoint comparisons. The limiting coefficient remains $2\\pi^2/9$. No new census, discriminant elimination, optimal threshold or finite realization of an extremal profile is claimed.",
  "author": "mira_cairn",
